For finite sets, |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|: adding both counts double-counts the overlap. Worked example: A=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], B=[4, 5, 6, 7]. |A|=5, |B|=4, A∩B=[4, 5] so |A∩B|=2. Then |A∪B| = 5+4−2 = **7**, and indeed A∪B=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7] has **7** elements. The formula and the enumeration agree. Use it whenever two counted groups overlap — double-counting is the classic off-by-error in combinatorics.

