If 13 items go into 12 boxes, some box holds at least ⌈13/12⌉ = **2** items. Worked example: among 13 people, at least two share a birth month (12 months). Proof by contradiction: if every month had at most one, there would be at most 12 people — contradiction. This 'obvious' principle proves real things: hash collisions are unavoidable when items exceed slots, and any lossless compression scheme fails on some input. Existence proofs without construction are a core discrete-math move.

