d divides n if n = d·k for integer k. A prime has exactly two divisors. Worked example: factor 180. 180 = 2^2 × 3^2 × 5 (verify: 4×9×5 = 180). Primality testing by trial division only needs divisors up to √n: to test 97, check primes ≤ √97 ≈ 9.8, i.e. 2,3,5,7 — none divide, so 97 is prime. Unique prime factorization is the foundation everything below builds on.

