Worked example with small primes p=61, q=53. n = 61×53 = **3233**; φ(n) = 60×52 = **3120**. Choose public exponent e=17 (coprime to φ). Private exponent d = e^(−1) mod φ = **2753**. Encrypt message 42: c = 42^17 mod 3233 = **2557**. Decrypt: m = 2557^2753 mod 3233 = **42** — the original message ✓. Every number here was computed, not asserted. Real RSA uses 2048-bit primes, but the mathematics is exactly this.

